pokeplatinum-wideweather.patch (tools/pokeplat_gen.py applies it to build/pokeplatinum, after widepointers6): the weather particles'
work array. Every particle node (ov5_021D5EB8.c UnkStruct_ov5_021D6FA8) carries a 10-word work array, unk_0C, that its weather keeps
its state in (unk_08 points at it). widepointers6 made two weathers keep pointers there as intptr_t (snow ov5_021D7C40 / ov5_021D7E20:
10 words, ashfall ov5_021DA5A0 / ov5_021DA6BC: 8 words), but the array stayed s32[10], 40 bytes: on the 64-bit host the snow's
v4[5..9] (and the ash's v5[5..7]) wrote past it over the node's list links unk_34 / unk_38 (v4[5] = 0, v4[6] = 0) and the next pool
node. The next walk of the list (ov5_021D7568, ov5_021D5EB8.c:2035 `v4 = v3->unk_34`) read a NULL link and SIGSEGV'd at the first
snow frame on every snowing map (pt_sweep: Snowpoint City, its Pokecenter and east house, Stark Mountain outside, Snowpoint Temple,
Route 227 and its house). The array is intptr_t[10] (an int's size on the DS, room for a pointer per word on the host; the s32 weathers
use its first half), and the size check and the clear use sizeof(unk_0C) instead of sizeof(s32) * 10.
--- a/src/overlay005/ov5_021D5EB8.c
+++ b/src/overlay005/ov5_021D5EB8.c
@@ -65,5 +65,5 @@
     Sprite *unk_04;
     void *unk_08;
-    s32 unk_0C[10];
+    intptr_t unk_0C[10];
     struct UnkStruct_ov5_021D6FA8_tag *unk_34;
     struct UnkStruct_ov5_021D6FA8_tag *unk_38;
@@ -1536,5 +1536,5 @@
     GF_ASSERT(param0->unk_08 == NULL);
     GF_ASSERT(param1 > 0);
-    GF_ASSERT(param1 <= (sizeof(s32) * 10));
+    GF_ASSERT(param1 <= sizeof(param0->unk_0C));
 
     param0->unk_08 = param0->unk_0C;
@@ -1544,5 +1544,5 @@
 {
     param0->unk_08 = NULL;
-    memset(param0->unk_0C, 0, (sizeof(s32) * 10));
+    memset(param0->unk_0C, 0, sizeof(param0->unk_0C));
 }
 
